  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "FHA financing provides government-backed home loans with low down payments (as low as 3.5%) and flexible credit requirements, making homeownership more accessible for first-time or low-to-moderate-income buyers. These loans require a minimum 580 credit score for maximum financing, mandate mortgage insurance (MIP), and must be used for a primary residence. Key FHA Loan Features Down Payment: Minimum of 3.5% for credit scores of 580 or higher; 10% for scores between 500-579. Credit Scores: Lower scores accepted compared to conventional loans (often as low as 500-580). Mortgage Insurance Premium (MIP): Requires both an upfront fee and annual premiums added to monthly payments. Property Eligibility: Must be a primary residence (no investment properties or second homes). Loan Limits: FHA sets maximum loan amounts based on your county's median home cost. Common Requirements Employment: Proof of steady income and two years of employment history. Debt-to-Income (DTI): Typically, a lower credit score requires a lower DTI ratio to qualify. Appraisal: The property must meet FHA minimum property standards regarding safety and structural integrity. FHA minimum property standards Common Uses Home Purchases: Single-family homes, manufactured housing, or approved condos. Renovation: The FHA 203(k) loan allows combining purchase and renovation costs. FHA 203(k) loan Refinancing: Includes \"streamline\" refinancing for current FHA borrowers. streamline\" refinancing Interested borrowers should consult approved lenders to get a personalized quote and compare total costs with conventional loans. personalized quote",
